findings.body:
The PR removed both the expectation and the wait from `testDownloadingSVGImage_withKingfisherProcessor_forStandardSVGCase`. `DefaultSiteImageDownloader.downloadImage` is asynchronous: Kingfisher fetches from the local GCDWebServer on a background queue. So the test method returns right after starting the request.

::code{file="BrowserKit/Tests/SiteImageViewTests/SVGImageProcessorTests.swift" lines="26-37"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The `XCTAssertEqual` / `XCTFail` calls in the callback either fire after the test has finished, where XCTest ignores them or blames another test, or never fire at all. This test now always passes, even if SVG parsing regresses. The other four tests in the file keep their expectation and switched to `wait(for:)`, which shows the intended pattern.

Restore the expectation, matching the sibling tests:

::suggestion{file="BrowserKit/Tests/SiteImageViewTests/SVGImageProcessorTests.swift" lines="26-37"}
        let exp = expectation(description: "Image download and parse")

        let siteDownloader = DefaultSiteImageDownloader()
        siteDownloader.downloadImage(with: mockedURL, options: [.processor(SVGImageProcessor())]) { result in
            switch result {
            case .success(let result):
                XCTAssertEqual(result.originalData, imageData)
                XCTAssertEqual(result.url, mockedURL)
                XCTAssertEqual(result.image.size, expectedRasterSize)
                exp.fulfill()
            case .failure(let error):
                XCTFail("Should not have an error: \(error) \(error.errorDescription ?? "")")
                exp.fulfill()
            }
        }

        wait(for: [exp], timeout: 2.0)

The expectation and `wait(for:)` were removed from the success-path test. `WKUIHandler.requestMediaCapturePermission` currently calls the handler synchronously, so the assertion does run today. But the test no longer checks that the handler is invoked at all. If someone changes the implementation to skip the handler or call it asynchronously, the test still passes. The two sibling tests (`IsActiveFalse`, `DelegateReturnsFalse`) kept their expectations, so this one is now inconsistent with them.

::code{file="BrowserKit/Tests/WebEngineTests/WKUIHandlerTests.swift" lines="35-43"}

`XCTestExpectation` is Sendable, so the `@Sendable` closure can still capture it. There was no concurrency reason to drop it:

::suggestion{file="BrowserKit/Tests/WebEngineTests/WKUIHandlerTests.swift" lines="35-43"}
    func testRequestMediaCaptureSuccess() {
        let subject = createSubject(isActive: true)

        let expectation = expectation(description: "Wait for the decision handler to be called")
        let decisionHandler: @Sendable (WKPermissionDecision) -> Void = { (decision: WKPermissionDecision) in
            XCTAssertEqual(decision, .prompt)
            expectation.fulfill()
        }

        subject.requestMediaCapturePermission(decisionHandler: decisionHandler)
        wait(for: [expectation])
    }

The diff deletes the three-line MPL 2.0 header at the top of this file. Every other source file in the repo has it, and Mozilla requires it on source files. Nothing about the Swift 6 migration calls for removing it; it looks accidental.

